vents outliers from dominating the learning process.\ndf['sqrt_e'] = np.sqrt(df['e'])\n\n# log_branchCount\n# Logarithm transformation of branch count.\n# Usefulness: Branch count can be linked to defect-prone modules, but its wide range might mask this relationship. The log transformation enables better numeric stability.\ndf['log_branchCount'] = np.log(df['branchCount'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Create statistical aggregations  \nAggregate features can help capture relationships and interactions between features, offering the model higher-level insights.\n\npython\n# avg_complexity\n# Average of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)), essential complexity (ev(g)), and design complexity (iv(g)).\n# Usefulness: Aggregating multiple complexity metrics provides a consolidated view of overall complexity, which may correlate strongly with software defects.\ndf['avg_complexity'] = (df['v(g)'] + df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']) / 3\n\n# avg_lines\n# Average of lines of code (loc), code lines (lOCode), comment lines (lOComment), and blank lines (lOBlank).\n# Usefulness: This represents an aggregate view of all physical lines in a module, helping to evaluate its readability and maintainability, which can impact defect rates.\ndf['avg_lines'] = (df['loc'] + df['lOCode'] + df['lOComment'] + df['lOBlank']) / 4\n\n# uniq_op_to_total_op_ratio\n# Ratio of unique operators (uniq_Op) to total operators (total_Op).\n# Usefulness: This ratio represents diversity in usage of operators within a module. Higher ratios may indicate more non-traditional or complex code structures, which can be harder to maintain and debug.\ndf['uniq_op_to_total_op_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Dropping Columns\nThese columns are redundant or less informative (based on prior failed attempts).\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n# This column has extremely low variance (mean = 0.1146, std = 0.6869), suggesting it conveys minimal information about defect prediction.\n\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\n# Comment line counts are predominantly zero (median = 0), and models might struggle to learn from such sparse/discrete data.\n\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# Blank line counts are similarly sparse (median = 0); this feature likely has weak predictive power for defect prediction.\nend\n\n### Summary\nThe new features (ratios, transformations, statistical aggregations) are intuitive and grounded in software quality metrics. They focus on revealing cross-feature relationships (ratios), mitigating skew (log/sqrt), and integrating feature groups (aggregations). Dropping redundant columns ensures the model isn't cluttered with noise.", "python\n# Feature: operator_operand_density_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of unique operators to unique operands, as uniq_Op / (uniq_Opnd + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This measures the dense usage of operators compared to operands, which may reveal modules with unconventional or overly complex logic, potentially indicating higher defect proneness.\n\ndf['operator_operand_density_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: adjusted_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Combines logical and design complexity, adjusted for essential complexity, as v(g) / (iv(g) * ev(g) + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This extends the complexity_ratio to include essential complexity, providing a holistic view of how logical, design, and essential aspects interact. Modules with higher values may indicate overly intricate designs prone to defects.\n\ndf['adjusted_complexity_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['iv(g)'] * df['ev(g)'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: density_blank_lines_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of blank lines to