###["### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n#### Step 1: Generate interaction features using ratios  \nInteraction features capture relationships between existing features, providing additional context for the model to learn potential correlations and dependencies.\n\npython\n# loc_vg_ratio\n# Ratio of lines of code (loc) to cyclomatic complexity (v(g)).\n# Usefulness: This represents how dense or complex a module's code is per line of code, potentially revealing modules with high complexity relative to their size. This can uncover defect-prone modules.\ndf['loc_vg_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-6)\n\n# vg_ev_ratio\n# Ratio of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) to essential complexity (ev(g)).\n# Usefulness: This highlights modules where the logical structure's cyclomatic complexity is disproportionate to the essential complexity, potentially indicating poor design or maintenance issues.\ndf['vg_ev_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['ev(g)'] + 1e-6)\n\n# loc_n_ratio\n# Ratio of lines of code (loc) to Halstead's total operators + operands (n).\n# Usefulness: Modules with high operator/operand density relative to the lines of code might be harder to read, maintain, or debug, correlating with potential defects.\ndf['loc_n_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['n'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Apply transformations like logarithm and square root  \nTransforms can help reduce skewness in features with large ranges or high variance, enabling better feature scaling and meaningful comparisons.\n\npython\n# log_v\n# Logarithm transformation of Halstead 'volume'.\n# Usefulness: Halstead's volume (v) spans a large range, skewing toward higher values. Applying a log transformation reduces this skew, allowing the model to better identify patterns.\ndf['log_v'] = np.log(df['v'] + 1e-6)\n\n# sqrt_e\n# Square root transformation of Halstead 'effort'.\n# Usefulness: Halstead 'effort' (e) has a very large range with extreme values. The square root transformation stabilizes variance and prevents outliers from dominating the learning process.\ndf['sqrt_e'] = np.sqrt(df['e'])\n\n# log_branchCount\n# Logarithm transformation of branch count.\n# Usefulness: Branch count can be linked to defect-prone modules, but its wide range might mask this relationship. The log transformation enables better numeric stability.\ndf['log_branchCount'] = np.log(df['branchCount'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Create statistical aggregations  \nAggregate features can help capture relationships and interactions between features, offering the model higher-level insights.\n\npython\n# avg_complexity\n# Average of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)), essential complexity (ev(g)), and design complexity (iv(g)).\n# Usefulness: Aggregating multiple complexity metrics provides a consolidated view of overall complexity, which may correlate strongly with software defects.\ndf['avg_complexity'] = (df['v(g)'] + df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']) / 3\n\n# avg_lines\n# Average of lines of code (loc), code lines (lOCode), comment lines (lOComment), and blank lines (lOBlank).\n# Usefulness: This represents an aggregate view of all physical lines in a module, helping to evaluate its readability and maintainability, which can impact defect rates.\ndf['avg_lines'] = (df['loc'] + df['lOCode'] + df['lOComment'] + df['lOBlank']) / 4\n\n# uniq_op_to_total_op_ratio\n# Ratio of unique operators (uniq_Op) to total operators (total_Op).\n# Usefulness: This ratio represents diversity in usage of operators within a module. Higher ratios may indicate more non-traditional or complex code structures, which can be harder to maintain and debug.\ndf['uniq_op_to_total_op_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n#### Dropping Columns\nThese columns are redundant or less informative (based on prior failed attempts).\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n# This column has extremely low variance (mean = 0.1146, std = 0.6869), suggesting it conveys minimal information about defect prediction.\n\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\n# Comment line counts are predominantly zero (median = 0), and models might struggle to learn from such sparse/discrete data.\n\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# Blank line counts are similarly sparse (median = 0); this feature likely has weak predictive power for defect prediction.\nend\n\n### Summary\nThe new features (ratios, transformations, statistical aggregations) are intuitive and grounded in software quality metrics. They focus on revealing cross-feature relationships (ratios), mitigating skew (log/sqrt), and integrating feature groups (aggregations). Dropping redundant columns ensures the model isn't cluttered with noise.", "python\n# Feature: operator_operand_density_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of unique operators to unique operands, as uniq_Op / (uniq_Opnd + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This measures the dense usage of operators compared to operands, which may reveal modules with unconventional or overly complex logic, potentially indicating higher defect proneness.\n\ndf['operator_operand_density_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: adjusted_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Combines logical and design complexity, adjusted for essential complexity, as v(g) / (iv(g) * ev(g) + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This extends the complexity_ratio to include essential complexity, providing a holistic view of how logical, design, and essential aspects interact. Modules with higher values may indicate overly intricate designs prone to defects.\n\ndf['adjusted_complexity_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['iv(g)'] * df['ev(g)'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: density_blank_lines_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of blank lines to code lines (lOBlank / (lOCode + 1)), a signal for readability and documentation within a module.\n# Usefulness: Modules with high blank line ratios may indicate better spacing, readability, or intentional separation of logic, which could correlate with lower defect incidence. Conversely, overly dense modules may be harder to debug.\n\ndf['density_blank_lines_ratio'] = df['lOBlank'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# The column l (Halstead 'program length') has a very limited range ([0.0, 2.0]) and is already captured indirectly through loc_density and complexity metrics, reducing its standalone contribution to defect prediction.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# Essential complexity (ev(g)) has low variance across the dataset and is primarily constant (Median = Q1 = Q3 = 1.0). This limits its usefulness as a standalone feature.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column uniq_Opnd is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['uniq_Opnd'], inplace=True)\n# The column uniq_Opnd (unique operands) overlaps conceptually with uniq_Op and loc_density. Interaction features (such as operator_operand_density_ratio) already account for its relationships, making it redundant for the task.\nend", "python\n# Feature: loc_efficiency\n# Feature description: Computes the efficiency of lines of code per unique operator and operand as loc / (uniq_Op * uniq_Opnd + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights how effectively lines of code are using operators and operands. Modules with lower efficiency may indicate redundancies, inefficiencies, or increased defect likelihood.\ndf['loc_efficiency'] = df['loc'] / (df['uniq_Op'] * df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: operator_diversity\n# Feature description: Ratio of unique operators to total operators (loc / (uniq_Op + 1)).\n# Usefulness: This feature shows the variety of operators used in a module relative to the total number of operations. Higher diversity might correlate with better modularity, while low diversity can indicate overly repetitive code with potential defects.\ndf['operator_diversity'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['loc'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: blank_code_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of blank lines (lOBlank) to total lines of code (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: This feature reflects code cleanliness and organization. Too many blank lines may represent poor structure or excessive spacing, which could indirectly correlate with higher defect rates (e.g., difficulty maintaining code).\ndf['blank_code_ratio'] = df['lOBlank'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# ev(g) (essential complexity) has extremely low variance (Q1 = 1, Median = 1, Q3 = 1), meaning that it offers minimal discrimination across modules. Its predictive utility for the defect classification task is likely limited.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# l (Halstead program length) has a very narrow range ([0.0, 2.0]) and low variance. This restricts its ability to provide meaningful differentiation for defect prediction across different modules.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column loc_density is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['loc_density'], inplace=True)\n# loc_density already reflects lines of code density calculations, but other added features (e.g., loc_efficiency and operator_diversity) expand on it and provide a deeper understanding of relationships, making loc_density redundant.\nend", "python\n# Feature: operator_to_operand_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of unique operators to unique operands, as uniq_Op / (uniq_Opnd + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This ratio provides insight into the balance of operators and operands in the code. Modules with skewed ratios might indicate overly complex or unconventional logic, which may correlate with higher defect likelihood.\ndf['operator_to_operand_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: blank_to_code_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of blank lines to code lines, as lOBlank / (lOCode + 1).\n# Usefulness: This feature examines the organization and readability of code. Poorly structured or overly spaced modules might correlate with higher defect rates or maintenance challenges.\ndf['blank_to_code_ratio'] = df['lOBlank'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: loc_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of lines of code (loc) to design complexity (iv(g) + 1).\n# Usefulness: Measures the density of code within complex modules and provides insight into whether complexity is justified given the lines of code. High values could indicate overcomplicated, defect-prone code structures.\ndf['loc_complexity_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['iv(g)'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The column l (Halstead 'program length') has a narrow range ([0.0, 2.0]) and limited variability (Mean = 0.3163, Std = 0.3337). Its information is indirectly captured by other features such as loc_density and loc_efficiency.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Essential complexity (ev(g)) has very low variance (Q1 = Median = Q3 = 1.0). This makes it less useful for the task of discriminating defect-prone modules, especially since other complexity metrics like v(g) and iv(g) provide detailed insights.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Blank line counts are sparse (Median = Q1 = 0.0). Since blank_to_code_ratio retains relevant information in relation to readability and spacing, the original feature is redundant.\nend", "python\n# Feature: operator_operand_interaction\n# Feature description: Computes the interaction between unique operators and unique operands, as uniq_Op * uniq_Opnd.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines the diversity of operators and operands, highlighting modules with high logical interaction. This could indicate complex logic where defects are more likely to arise.\ndf['operator_operand_interaction'] = df['uniq_Op'] * df['uniq_Opnd']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: normalized_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Adjusts the complexity_ratio by normalizing with v(g), as complexity_ratio / (v(g) + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This feature provides a more scaled view of how complexity compares to cyclomatic complexity. Modules with very high adjusted values may represent disproportionate complexities that are defect-prone.\ndf['normalized_complexity_ratio'] = df['complexity_ratio'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: code_efficiency\n# Feature description: Calculates the efficiency of code density as loc_density / (complexity_ratio + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This assesses how compactly operators and operands are used relative to complexity. Lower efficiency could indicate more defect-prone, dense or disorganized modules.\ndf['code_efficiency'] = df['loc_density'] / (df['complexity_ratio'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# ev(g) (essential complexity) exhibits low variance (Q1 = 1, Median = 1, Q3 = 1), providing limited differentiation across modules. The added feature normalized_complexity_ratio subsumes its utility.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# l (Halstead program length) has a narrow range ([0.0, 2.0]) and low variance, limiting its standalone value for defect prediction. Interactions and efficiency features provide richer context for the task.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column loc_density is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['loc_density'], inplace=True)\n# loc_density is reflected indirectly in the added feature code_efficiency, which incorporates complexity considerations alongside density, providing more comprehensive information.\nend", "### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n#### Step 1: Generate an interaction feature combining operators and operands\npython\n# Feature: operator_operand_interaction\n# Feature description: Computes the interaction between unique operators and unique operands using multiplication, as uniq_Op * uniq_Opnd.\n# Usefulness: This captures the logical interaction between diversity in operators and operands. Modules with higher logical interaction may indicate greater complexity, which might correlate with higher defect proneness.\n\ndf['operator_operand_interaction'] = df['uniq_Op'] * df['uniq_Opnd']\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Normalize Halstead's lines of code density\npython\n# Feature: normalized_loc_density\n# Feature description: Normalizes loc_density by adjusting for total code lines (lOCode), as loc_density / (lOCode + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: Normalization helps adjust for size disparities among modules. Modules with disproportionately high density relative to lines of code could signal more complex or tightly packed logic prone to defects.\n\ndf['normalized_loc_density'] = df['loc_density'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1)\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Generate a readability ratio\npython\n# Feature: readability_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of blank lines to total lines of code, including comments, as lOBlank / (lOCode + lOBlank + 1) (+1 to avoid division by zero).\n# Usefulness: This represents code readability and structure. Modules with better readability may be easier to maintain, reducing defect risks, while poorly structured modules may indicate higher defect rates.\n\ndf['readability_ratio'] = df['lOBlank'] / (df['lOCode'] + df['lOBlank'] + 1)\nend\n\n### Dropping Low Variance or Redundant Columns\n\n#### Drop Column 1: Essential complexity (ev(g))\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# Essential complexity has low variance (Q1 = Median = Q3 = 1.0), offering limited value for differentiating defect-prone modules. Complexity interaction features (e.g., operator_operand_interaction) subsume its utility.\nend\n\n#### Drop Column 2: Halstead program length (l)\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# The column l (Halstead program length) has a narrow range ([0.0, 2.0]) and low variance. Its conceptual meaning is already captured by loc_density and related interaction features.\nend\n\n#### Drop Column 3: Halstead count of blank lines (lOBlank)\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# Blank line counts have sparse distributions (Median = Q1 = 0). The new feature readability_ratio retains its useful context, making this column redundant.\nend", "python\n# Feature: loc_to_complexity_ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of lines of code (loc_density) to overall complexity (complexity_ratio), as loc_density / (complexity_ratio + 1).\n# Usefulness: This provides insight into how dense the code is in relation to its logical and design complexity. Modules with high ratios may indicate overly dense code prone to errors.\n\ndf['loc_to_complexity_ratio'] = df['loc_density'] / (df['complexity_ratio'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: operator_uniqueness_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of unique operators to unique operands, as uniq_Op / (uniq_Opnd + 1).\n# Usefulness: This feature shows the balance between operators and operands, where extreme ratios might indicate inefficiencies or unconventional code structures more prone to defects.\n\ndf['operator_uniqueness_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: adjusted_complexity_efficiency\n# Feature description: Computes efficiency based on logical complexity divided by the product of essential and design complexity, as complexity_ratio / (ev(g) * iv(g) + 1).\n# Usefulness: This adjusts complexity metrics for better insight into how logical complexity scales with essential and design complexities. Modules with low efficiency values may indicate poor optimization and higher defect risk.\n\ndf['adjusted_complexity_efficiency'] = df['complexity_ratio'] / (df['ev(g)'] * df['iv(g)'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ev(g) is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n# ev(g) (essential complexity) has extremely low variance across the dataset and does not provide meaningful differentiation (Median = Q1 = Q3 = 1.0). Additionally, adjusted_complexity_efficiency incorporates its utility.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n# The feature l (Halstead 'program length') has limited range ([0.0, 2.0]) and variability, making it redundant when richer features like loc_density and loc_to_complexity_ratio are incorporated.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\n# Blank line counts (lOBlank) are predominantly zero (Median = Q1 = 0.0), indicating sparse utility for predictive modeling. Moreover, it is indirectly captured in features focusing on module density and operator-operand relationships.\nend"]###,
